---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Investment Banking | Hayden Smith" description="Why investment banking, why TMT, and why Recalc.">
	<section class="wrap intro">
		<a class="back-link" href="/">&larr; Back to the beach</a>
		<p class="eyebrow">Investment Banking</p>
		<h1>Where I want to go next.</h1>
	</section>

	<section class="wrap body">
		<p class="big">
			Research taught me how to form a view. Investment banking is where that view gets tied to an actual client and deal, and has to hold up in front of people who have done this for years.
		</p>

		<p>
			The Rocket Lab DCF was my first valuation, and I built it from a blank spreadsheet and a stack of filings. The hardest part was deciding which assumptions I could actually defend once people started poking holes in them. Covering RDDT and FORM for the Tartan Student Fund has been the same exercise at a faster pace, figuring out what a business is worth when the comps don't fit neatly and being honest about what I'm least sure of.
		</p>

		<p>
			What I want from banking is to do that work on real transactions and learn how deals actually get done from people who are great at it. I've picked the harder path enough times to know it suits me. I added a statistics major on top of a finance concentration, went through the IB Academy's bank-style interviews, and kept a 3.9 while working restaurant jobs. Those jobs taught me more than people might expect. Running a station through a 200-order night or coordinating 14 people between the front and back of house means getting things right quickly while other people depend on you, which is a big part of an analyst's job.
		</p>

		<p>
			The sector I'm most interested in is TMT, mostly because of the two stocks I've pitched. Reddit and FormFactor are about as different as two TMT companies get. Reddit is a consumer platform still figuring out how to make money from its users and its data, while FormFactor makes the equipment chipmakers use to test their chips during production. Working on both showed me how quickly changes in a TMT business show up in its numbers, and TMT is also one of the busiest sectors for deals, which is a big part of why I want to cover it as a banker.
		</p>

		<p>
			That's why I joined Carnegie Mellon's Investment Banking Academy, a mentee program with an interview process modeled on how banks actually recruit. I was selected from a pool of 50+ undergraduates. The weekly workshops cover accounting, financial statement analysis, and building DCF and merger models from a blank sheet, and then we practice defending them in mock interviews with mentors. I would rather be the least experienced person in a room who is actually doing the work than the most experienced person in the room who isn't.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="wrap body recalc">
		<h2>Why Recalc</h2>

		<p>
			Recalc looks for work ethic over pedigree, and that describes my path pretty well. I started working in 2021 because there wasn't much financial cushion at home, kept a job through high school, and have kept working through college while carrying two majors and a 3.9. Hard work was the one thing available to me that didn't cost money, and it's still what I rely on most.
		</p>

		<p>
			I also didn't get into finance through family connections. I found my own way in, first through the Introductory Finance Society and then through the Tartan Student Fund and the IB Academy. A program built to open this industry up to students like me is exactly what I've been looking for.
		</p>

		<p>
			I think I'd get a lot out of it, too. I've already built a DCF from scratch and pitched stocks, mistakes included, so Recalc's technical training would build on real experience instead of starting from zero. What I want most is what I can't get on my own, which is mentors who will push back on a weak assumption and a group of people working toward the same goal. As for the commitment the program asks for, I finish what I sign up for, whether that's a Greek Sing production with a 15-person crew or keeping a 100+ member chapter connected.
		</p>
	</section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
	.intro {
		padding-block: 1rem 2rem;
	}

	.eyebrow {
		margin: 1.5rem 0 1rem;
	}

	.body {
		padding-block: 1rem 2rem;
	}

	.big {
		font-family: var(--font-display);
		font-size: 1.4rem;
		font-weight: 500;
		max-width: 42ch;
		margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
	}

	.body p:not(.big) {
		font-size: 1.05rem;
		max-width: 60ch;
	}

	.recalc {
		border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
		padding-block: 2.5rem 3rem;
	}

	.recalc h2 {
		font-size: 1.6rem;
		margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
	}
</style>
